2026-06-30 15:25:45,456 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-ef6bcfac-999a-4e61-a2a9-ce782d582a52', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nF1_20200306_HS_1069_A_10_1.raw\nF1_20200306_HS_1069_A_10_2.raw\nF1_20200306_HS_1069_A_11_1.raw\nF1_20200306_HS_1069_A_11_2.raw\nF1_20200306_HS_1069_A_12_1.raw\nF1_20200306_HS_1069_A_12_2.raw\nF1_20200306_HS_1069_A_1_1a.raw\nF1_20200306_HS_1069_A_1_2.raw\nF1_20200306_HS_1069_A_2_1.raw\nF1_20200306_HS_1069_A_2_2.raw\nF1_20200306_HS_1069_A_3_1.raw\nF1_20200306_HS_1069_A_3_2.raw\nF1_20200306_HS_1069_B_10_1.raw\nF1_20200306_HS_1069_B_10_2.raw\nF1_20200306_HS_1069_B_11_1.raw\nF1_20200306_HS_1069_B_11_2.raw\nF1_20200306_HS_1069_B_12_1.raw\nF1_20200306_HS_1069_B_12_2.raw\nF1_20200306_HS_1069_B_1_1.raw\nF1_20200306_HS_1069_B_1_2.raw\nF1_20200306_HS_1069_B_2_1.raw\nF1_20200306_HS_1069_B_2_2.raw\nF1_20200306_HS_1069_B_3_1.raw\nF1_20200306_HS_1069_B_3_2.raw\nF1_20200316_HS_1074_C_10_1.raw\nF1_20200316_HS_1074_C_10_2.raw\nF1_20200316_HS_1074_C_11_1.raw\nF1_20200316_HS_1074_C_11_2.raw\nF1_20200316_HS_1074_C_12_1.raw\nF1_20200316_HS_1074_C_12_2.raw\nF1_20200316_HS_1074_C_1_1a.raw\nF1_20200316_HS_1074_C_1_2a.raw\nF1_20200316_HS_1074_C_2_1a.raw\nF1_20200316_HS_1074_C_2_2a.raw\nF1_20200316_HS_1074_C_3_1a.raw\nF1_20200316_HS_1074_C_3_2a.raw\nF1_20200316_HS_1074_D_10_1.raw\nF1_20200316_HS_1074_D_10_2.raw\nF1_20200316_HS_1074_D_11_1.raw\nF1_20200316_HS_1074_D_11_2.raw\nF1_20200316_HS_1074_D_12_1.raw\nF1_20200316_HS_1074_D_12_2.raw\nF1_20200316_HS_1074_D_1_1a.raw\nF1_20200316_HS_1074_D_1_2a.raw\nF1_20200316_HS_1074_D_2_1a.raw\nF1_20200316_HS_1074_D_2_2a.raw\nF1_20200316_HS_1074_D_3_1a.raw\nF1_20200316_HS_1074_D_3_2a.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nAPEX2 proximity labeling of PI(3)P dependent ER and early endosome membrane contact tether proteins\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nTo understand the nature of ER and early endosome membrane contact site, we capitalized the MTM1 knock out (KO) HeLa cells that do not dissociate the ER and early endosome membrane contact upon nutrient starvation. By using the Doxycyclin inducible APEX2 fused Rab5A (early endosome marker) expressing stable cell lines of wildtype (WT) or KO cells, early endsome proximity proteins were acutely biotinyated during starvation. Subsequent biochemical fractionation were conducted to enrich ER membrane and biotinylated proteins were furhter enriched for Mass spectrometry analysis.\n\n[METHODS]\nHeLa WT or MTM1 KO cell line stably expressing doxycycline-inducible 3xHA-APEX2-Rab5A were seeded in 15 cm dishes with 17x10^5 cells (WT=6 dishes, KO=3 dishes) in the presence of doxycycline for 16 h prior to biotinylation. The next day cells at approximately 85% confluency were starved (EBSS, 2hr) and treated with 500 μM phenol-biotin and hydrogen peroxide to induce biotinylation, quenched, washed once with isolation buffer (225 mM mannitol, 75 mM sucrose, 30 mM Tris-HCl pH 7.4, 0.1 mM EGTA), and, finally, scraped into 1 ml of the same buffer resulting in approx. 2 ml total volume. 0.5 ml of this sample were saved (whole cell lysate, WCL) as a control. The remaining 1.5ml were homogenized using a 2 ml dounce homogenizer (approx. 100-120 strokes) on ice in the presence of protease and phosphatase inhibitors. To isolate light membranes containing the ER, the material was centrifuged twice at 600xg for 2 min each. The pellet (F1) including nuclei and cell debris was resuspended in 150 μl RIPA buffer. The remaining supernatant was centrifuged twice at 7,000xg for 20 min each. The resulting pellet (F2) including the mitochondrial fraction was resuspended in 150 μl RIPA buffer and further fractionated by centrifugation at 20,000xg for 60 min to pellet light ER membranes (F3). This ER-enriched membrane fraction was resuspended in150 μl RIPA buffer. Protein concentration was determined using the Bradford assay. To isolate biotinylated proteins, 130 μg of WCL or F3 fraction were rotated in the presence of Streptavidin Magnetic Beads. Eluted biotinylated proteins were reduced (5mM DTT, 30 min at 55°C), alkylated (15mM iodacetamide, 20 min at RT in the dark) and were submitted to LC-MS analysis (Samples from two independent experiments).\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
